B-0 (Boosty) -- stdio input keeps musl's pushback invariant: fscanf works, and
getc stops costing a syscall per byte.

0002 replaced musl's readv-based __stdio_read with "a single SYS_read straight
into the caller's buffer" and called the dropped half "a throughput
optimization, not a semantic one". It was both, and the semantic half was
load-bearing:

  * __toread() parks the stream at rpos == rend == buf + buf_size.
  * Upstream's read then REFILLS f->buf and returns the requested byte from
    it, so the byte just handed out sits at f->rpos[-1].
  * The scan helpers lean on exactly that: shunget() is a bare `rpos--`, and
    __shgetc() stores the byte itself only when `rpos <= buf` (the unbuffered
    case, into the UNGET area).

With 0002 the byte went straight to the caller, rpos stayed at buf + buf_size,
and every shunget() stepped back onto buf[buf_size-1] -- a stale byte, usually
NUL. So on every real FILE, the scanf family failed at the first pushed-back
delimiter: fscanf(f, "%7s %d %d") over "alpha 12 -7" returned 1 with an empty
word. sscanf was never affected (a string pseudo-FILE has its own read). And
because the buffer never filled, every getc/fgets/getline byte was its own
SYS_read.

Found 2026-09-21 by the 0034 device pin: pouch-hello-malloc parses /ctl/memory
with fscanf and got 0. Ground truth before theory -- raw read(1), read(64),
fgetc and fread over the same file all returned the right bytes, which cleared
the kernel and devctl and left only the scan path.

The fix needs no readv. Upstream's one-byte-request arm on a buffered stream
was ALREADY a plain SYS_read into f->buf; this restores it, and generalises it
to every request no larger than the buffer: read into f->buf, serve the caller
from it, keep the surplus as readahead. So a run of small fread()s (TyrQuake
reads its pak header and every demo message length this way: common/common.c,
NQ/cl_demo.c) costs one syscall per buffer-full, as it does upstream, instead
of one per call; on a 9P-backed file that is a round trip each. A request LARGER than the buffer, and any
unbuffered stream, stays a direct read into the caller: no double copy, and no
pushback slot needed (fread never ungets; an unbuffered stream takes
__shgetc's buf[-1] path; ungetc() stores its byte itself). One SYS_read per
call in both arms -- never a second read after a successful first, which would
block a pipe or tty that had already delivered what it had. Readahead survives
exactly where upstream's does: fread's loop calls __toread() (which drops the
buffer) only after a SHORT count, and a short count leaves no surplus.

Behaviour changes to know about -- stdio input now reads AHEAD (up to BUFSIZ),
as it does on every other libc, and everything that follows from that is now
true here too:
  * A program that mixes FILE reads with raw reads of the same fd, or hands
    the fd to a child mid-stream, sees standard POSIX readahead instead of the
    accidental byte-exact positioning 0002 gave it.
  * fflush(stdin), fclose() and exit() on a NON-SEEKABLE stream (a pipe, a
    tty, a socket) discard whatever was read ahead; on a seekable one they
    seek the fd back, as upstream does.
  * An update stream ("r+") that reads and then writes WITHOUT the fseek or
    fflush ISO C requires between them writes at the readahead offset, not at
    the logical position. Conforming programs are unaffected.
  * Bytes read through a FILE now persist in its buffer until overwritten or
    freed. A program that reads a SECRET with fgets() and wipes only its own
    copy leaves one in the FILE (Stratum's janusd reads a passphrase this way:
    src/janus/janusd.c). True on every libc; new here. setvbuf(f, 0, _IONBF, 0)
    before the read is the portable way to opt out.
  * TTY TYPE-AHEAD: a canonical-mode read should return at most one line, and
    neither tty layer bounds it today (kernel/cons.c and ptyfs' ring_drain
    both drain past the newline). 0002's byte-at-a-time reads hid that; a
    BUFSIZ read now takes the whole type-ahead into the first reader's FILE,
    so a line typed ahead for the NEXT program is consumed by this one. The
    defect is the tty layers' (POSIX ICANON), tracked as its own item; recorded
    here because this patch is what makes it observable.

The arm boundary is `len > buf_size`, and the first draft of the generalised
arm had `>=`. A host model of the read state machine (the audit's, extended
with this arm; random short reads, buffer sizes 0/1/2/3/7/16/64/1024, mixed
getc / fread / ungetc / scan / peek) failed 1889 of 2000 trials at
buf_size == 1 and none anywhere else: a one-byte request on a one-byte buffer
took the DIRECT arm and lost the pushback slot -- the original 0002 defect,
reintroduced at one size. With `>`: 0 of 32000, against 26496 of 32000 for
the 0002 backend on the same trials. pouch-hello-fopen's scan leg now runs a
second pass on a setvbuf() buffer of UNGET+1 bytes to hold that boundary on
the device.

Device-side pin (same commit, not in this patch file): pouch-hello-fopen gains
a `scan` leg -- three pushbacks deep, checked on VALUES -- measured RED on the
unfixed backend (n=1, w1=[]) before this patch was applied.

--- a/src/stdio/__stdio_read.c
+++ b/src/stdio/__stdio_read.c
@@ -1,17 +1,58 @@
 #include "stdio_impl.h"
+#include <string.h>
 
 /* pouch: musl's __stdio_read issues one readv over {caller buffer, the
-   stream's prefetch buffer}; Thylacine has no readv. pouch does a single
-   SYS_read straight into the caller's buffer. The dropped half is the
-   readahead into f->buf - a throughput optimization, not a semantic one:
-   the stream simply refills on the next read. F_EOF / F_ERR are set
-   exactly as musl's original does (cnt < 0 -> F_ERR, cnt == 0 -> F_EOF). */
+   stream's prefetch buffer}; Thylacine has no readv. Two arms, ONE SYS_read
+   each -- never a second read after a successful first, which would block an
+   interactive pipe or tty that has already delivered what it had.
+
+   The buffered arm: a request no larger than the stream's buffer reads into
+   f->buf and is served FROM it; the surplus stays as readahead. For the
+   one-byte request -- __uflow, so every getc/fgets/getline/scanf -- this is
+   upstream's own arm (it never used readv there), and it keeps the invariant
+   the scan helpers lean on: after a read the returned byte sits at
+   f->rpos[-1], so shunget()'s bare `rpos--` re-reads it. 0002 read the byte
+   straight into the caller and left rpos == rend == buf + buf_size, so every
+   pushback re-read buf[buf_size-1] (stale, usually NUL): fscanf/scanf on a
+   real FILE failed at the first pushed-back delimiter, and every getc was
+   its own syscall. For 1 < len <= buf_size -- fread's remainder loop on a
+   small record -- it is what upstream's readv bought: one syscall fills the
+   buffer and the next small freads are served from memory, where a direct
+   read would make each its own syscall (on a 9P-backed file, a round trip).
+
+   The direct arm (a request LARGER than the buffer, or any unbuffered
+   stream) reads straight into the caller: no double copy, and no pushback
+   slot is needed -- fread never ungets, and an unbuffered stream takes
+   __shgetc's buf[-1] path. The boundary is `>`, not `>=`: a one-byte request
+   on a ONE-byte buffer (setvbuf with UNGET+1 bytes) must still take the
+   buffered arm, or the scan helpers lose their pushback slot again.
+
+   A short count is returned as short; fread's loop calls again, exactly as
+   it does after upstream's short readv.
+
+   F_EOF / F_ERR exactly as upstream: cnt < 0 -> F_ERR, cnt == 0 -> F_EOF. */
 size_t __stdio_read(FILE *f, unsigned char *buf, size_t len)
 {
-	long cnt = syscall(SYS_read, f->fd, buf, len);
+	long cnt;
+	size_t k;
+
+	if (!f->buf_size || len > f->buf_size) {
+		cnt = syscall(SYS_read, f->fd, buf, len);
+		if (cnt <= 0) {
+			f->flags |= cnt ? F_ERR : F_EOF;
+			return 0;
+		}
+		return (size_t)cnt;
+	}
+
+	cnt = syscall(SYS_read, f->fd, f->buf, f->buf_size);
 	if (cnt <= 0) {
 		f->flags |= cnt ? F_ERR : F_EOF;
 		return 0;
 	}
-	return (size_t)cnt;
+	k = (size_t)cnt < len ? (size_t)cnt : len;
+	memcpy(buf, f->buf, k);
+	f->rpos = f->buf + k;
+	f->rend = f->buf + cnt;
+	return k;
 }
